<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=voltaic-pile">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Voltaic pile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The voltaic pile is a stack of paired metal discs separated by brine-soaked cloth or cardboard, the first electrical battery that could continuously provide an electric current; it was invented by the Italian chemist Alessandro <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a>, who published his experiments in 1799 and described the device to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 20 March 1800. It soon enabled the electrolysis of water, in 1800, and Humphry Davy's isolation of sodium and potassium in 1807. Batteries related to Volta's powered the 19th-century electrical industry until the dynamo arrived in the 1870s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile.jpg" alt="Voltaic pile" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 3.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Volta_battery-MHS_373-IMG_3840-gradient.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
